1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Vamana From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia See Kāśikāvṛttī for the medieval grammarians. Vamana
2 CONT
2 CONT Four-armed Vamana Devanagari	वामन Sanskrit Transliteration	Vāmana Affiliation	Avatar of Vishnu Weapon	Wooden umbrella and water pot Vamana (Devanagari: वामन, IAST: Vāmana) is described in the Puranas as the fifth Avatar of Vishnu,
2 CONC  and the first incarnation of the Second Age or Treta yuga.[1] He is the first Avatar to appear without anthropomorphic features, although as a dwarf Brahmin. He is also known as Upendra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vamana was born to Aditi and Kashyapa.[2] He is the twelfth of the Adityas. Vamana is also the younger brother of Indra. Hinduism Vamana as Trivikrama, depicted having three legs, one on the earth, a second raised in the heavens a
2 CONC nd a third on Bali's head. The Bhagavata Purana describes that Vishnu descended as the Vamana avatar to restore the authority of Indra over the heavens, as it had been taken by Mahabali, a benevolent Asura King. Bali was the grand
2 CONC son of Prahlada, the son of Hiranyakshipu. Vamana, in the guise of a short Brahman carrying a wooden umbrella, went to the king to request three paces of land. Mahabali consented, against the warning of his guru, Sukracharya. Vama
2 CONC na then revealed his identity and enlarged to gigantic proportions to stride over the three worlds. He stepped from heaven to earth with the first step, from earth to the netherworld with the second. King Mahabali, unable to fulfi
2 CONC ll his promise, offered his head for the third. Vamana then placed his foot and gave the king immortality for his humility. In worshiping Mahabali and his ancestor Prahláda, he conceded sovereignty of Pátála, the netherworld. Some
2 CONC  texts also report that Vamana did not step into the netherworld, and instead gave its rule to Bali. In giant form, Vamana is known as Trivikrama.[3] Just before King Mahavali was pushed out of this earth, he was given permission 
2 CONC by Vamana to visit his people once a year. The Onam festival is a celebration of welcoming Mahabali home to his lost kingdom. During this festival, beautiful floral decorations are made in every house and boat races are held throu
2 CONC ghout Kerala. A twenty-one-course feast is the most important part of the Onam festival.[3] [edit]Jainism Jainism also describes Vamana.[citation needed] In its scripture, an ill natured man has contention with Jain Munis, which r
2 CONC esults in the king banishing him from the kingdom. He traveled to a rival kingdom and managed to gain the favor and trust of its king, and is granted a wish. He asked to be made King for 4 months during Chaturmas, a holy time for 
2 CONC the Jains. The evil man orders that he must be allowed a bite of every meal in Jain Muni, thus not allowing them not eat for the rest of the day as the food would be considered contaminated. The Jain Shravaks and Shravikas, recogn
2 CONC izing his intent, consulted Vamana, the dwarf and avatar who had the capability to change his size. Dressed up as a tiny Brahman, he went to the house of the evil Raja while he performed a pooja. Near its end, the practitioner mus
2 CONC t give something to those present. He asked the dwarf what he wished, and Varana replied that wantedthree feet of land. The King consented. Vamana then grew so large that one foot covered the planet, another the heavens, and so th
2 CONC ere was no where to place the third. The evil king then realized his mistake, and in shame bowed his head down for the third footstep of Vamana. [edit]Symbolism
2 CONT
2 CONT Vamana avatar with King Mahabali Vamana taught King Mahabali that pride should be abandoned for advancement in life, and that wealth should be appreciated as it can easily disappear. Vamana then took the form of Mahavishnu and was
2 CONC  pleased by King Mahabali's determination to keep his promise, despite the curse of his spiritual master and losing his wealth. Vishnu named the King Mahabali since he was a Mahatma (great soul). He allowed Mahabali to return to t
2 CONC he spiritual sky to join Prahlada, the demoniac Hiranyakashipu's pious son, and other divine beings. Mahavishnu also declared that Mahabali would be able to rule the universe in the following yuga. Mahabali was the grandson of Pra
2 CONC hlada, with his father being Virochana, who was killed in a battle with the Devas. Mahabali is supposed to return every year to the land of his people, to ensure that they are prosperous. [edit]In Sikhism Vamana is discussed in th
2 CONC e Guru Granth Sahib, the sacred text of Sikhism.[4] Satjugi tai manio Chhalio bali bavan bhaio In Satyayuga, you sported as the dwarf incarnation, and fooled Bali. On page 1390 of the Guru Granth Sahib, Vamana is mentioned as the 
2 CONC "enticer" of Baliraja.[5] In the Ramayana
2 CONT
2 CONT Dwarf Vamana avatar at Rani ki vav, Patan, Gujarat According to the Adhyatma Ramayana It is also said that Vamana is the guard of the gate of Bali's underworld realm Sutala[6][7] and will remain so forever.[8] Tulsidas' Ramayana t
2 CONC oo declares that Vamana became the "dwarpal" (gate-defender) of Bali.[9] It is said that Mahabali attained Moksha by atmanivedinam.[10] Krishna in the Sri Rūpa Gosvāmī's Bhakti-rasāmrta-sindhuh[11] says that Mahabali came to Him o
2 CONC r attained Him. Some traditions also hold that Vamana was an avatar of Ganesha.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vamana (Devanagari: वामन, IAST: Vāmana) is described in the Puranic texts of Hinduism as the Fifth Avatara of Vishnu, and the first incarnation of the Second Age, or the Treta yuga. Also he is the first Avatar of Vishnu which appe
2 CONC ars with a completely human form, though it was that of a dwarf brahmin. He is also sometimes known as Upendra.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vamana
