1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -- The giant, obese, p Kumbhakarna owerful ogre brother of Ravana. He is awaken and enlisted into the battle against Rama and the monkey army as a last resort by Ravana. Kumbhakarna yawns as he is roused from sleep
2 CONT
2 CONT KUMBAkarna (Telugu: కుంభకర్ణ/కుంభకర్ణుడు Kumbhakarṇa/kumbhakarṇudu,Tamil: கும்பகருணன் Kumbakarunan, Sanskrit: कुम्भकर्ण Kumbhakarṇa, Malay: Kumbakarna, Indonesian: Kumbhakarno, Burmese: Kumbikhanna, Thai: Kumphakan), is a rakshasa
2 CONC  and brother of Ravana in the Indian Ramayana epic. Despite his monstrous size and great hunger, he was described to be of good character, though he killed and ate many Hindu monks just to show his power. He was considered so piou
2 CONC s, intelligent and brave that Indra was jealous of him. Along with his brothers, Ravana and Vibhishana, he performed a major yajna and penance for Lord Brahma. When the time came for asking a boon (blessing) from Brahma, his tongu
2 CONC e was tied by goddess Saraswati (acting on Indra's request). So instead of asking "Indraasana" (seat of Indra), he asked for "Nidraasana" (bed for sleeping). It is also said he intended to ask for Nirdevatvam (annihilation of the 
2 CONC Devas), and instead asked for Nidravatvam (sleep). His request was granted. But his brother Ravana asked Brahma to undo this boon as it was in reality a curse. So Kumbhakarna slept, and was awake for six months, when he ate everyt
2 CONC hing in the vicinity, including humans. During the war, Ravana went into battle and was humiliated by Rama and his army. He decided he needed the help of his brother Kumbhakarna, who was awakened with great difficulty. He woke up 
2 CONC only after one thousand elephants walked over him. When he was informed of the circumstances of Ravana's war with Rama, he tried to convince Ravana that what he was doing was wrong.[1] However, he chose to fight in the battle due 
2 CONC to his loyalty to his brother. After becoming drunk, Kumbhakarna went into battle. He devastated Rama's army, injured Hanuman, and knocked Sugriva unconscious and took him as a prisoner but was killed by Rama.[2] When Ravana heard
2 CONC  of his brother's death, he fainted and proclaimed that he is truly doomed. Kumbhakarna had two sons, Kumbha and Nikumbha, who too fought in the war against Rama and were killed.[3] Kumbhakarna is certainly one of the most interes
2 CONC ting characters in the Ramayana epic. Whereas most characters perfectly embody either virtue or vice, Kumbhakarna is a more complex figure. Able to realize the mistakes and wrong-doings of Ravana, even trying to intervene and mode
2 CONC rate at certain times, he is unable to openly oppose him, feeling bound by his warrior ethics. Fighting on what he knows is the wrong side, he is ultimately killed in combat. When he was finally defeated, he annihilated in Lord Ra
2 CONC ma and got salvation.[4] The battle between Kumbhakarna and Rama is prominently featured in the Balinese Kecak dance. According to the Shiva Purana, Kumbhakarna had another son called Bheema who fled to Dakini - on the ranges of t
2 CONC he Sahyadri mountains with his mother Karkati. Bheema took an oath to destroy Lord Vishnu and began a campaign of terror based on a boon provided by Lord Brahma. When Bheema defeated a devotee of Lord Shiva and interrupted his pen
2 CONC ances, Lord Shiva destroyed Bheema and manifested himself at that location in the form of the Bheemashankar Jyotirlinga - one of the 12 jyotirlingas in existence.
