1 NOTE {geni:about_me} tory of Urvashi and Pururava Apsaras are the celestial maidens of Indian mythology. They are not to be confused with angels. They are extremely beautiful immortals who live in heaven and entertain the demi-Gods wit
2 CONC h their dancing and singing. Urvashi is one such apsara. There are many legends about her birth but the following one is most prevalent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once the revered sage Narnarayan was meditating in the holy shrine of Badrinath situated in the Himalayas. Indra, the king of the demi-Gods, did not want the sage to acquire divine powers through the meditation and sent two apsara
2 CONC s to distract him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The sage struck his thigh and created a woman so beautiful that Indra's apsaras were left matchless. This was Urvashi, named from ur, the Sanskrit word for thigh. After his meditation was complete the sage gifted Urvashi to Indra,
2 CONC  and she occupied the pride of place in Indra's court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Atri was one of Brahma's seven sons who married Bhadra. He later went on to conceive a son named Som or the Moon. Som was a very attractive young man and eloped with Sage Brahaspati's wife, Tara. Som and Tara gave birth to Buddh d
2 CONC uring the absence of Brahaspati. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Vaivasvata Manu's daughter Ila fell in love with Buddh. They together conceived a son named Pururuva. Pururuva became a great king and established the Lunar Dynasty named after his grandfather. Ages later, in his incarnation as Kr
2 CONC ishna, Vishnu took birth in this dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Urvashi found the atmosphere in heaven stifling. Everything was cold and synthetic including the colours and the fragrances of the flowers. Urvashi often stole to the Earth at night with her friends to feel the wet dew under her f
2 CONC eet and the soft breeze against her body. On the other hand Pururava envied the Gods.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a regular invitee to Indra's court and was haunted at night by the grandeur he saw there. He would then take his chariot above the clouds and hurtle through the skies at break-neck speed. It was on such an occasion that the
2 CONC  two met.
2 CONT
2 CONT Urvashi was returning to heaven just before dawn with the other apsaras, when she was abducted by a demon. Pururava saw this and chased the demon on his chariot and freed Urvashi from his clutches. The brief period their bodies to
2 CONC uched changed their lives forever. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For the first time Urvasi experienced the warm flesh of a mortal, for the first time she heard blood pounding in veins and for the first time she heard the inhalation and exhalation of breath. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pururava had seen Urvashi in Indra's court before and to actually be close to the most beautiful woman in heaven gave him a high. Pururava left Urvashi with her friends but when they parted each was madly in love with the other bu
2 CONC t unsure whether the love was being reciprocated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Urvashi was a woman in a man's world and in keeping with the tradition of the times expected the man to make the first move. Pururava on the other hand feared rejection because he did not expect the pride of heaven to come and liv
2 CONC e with a mortal, and hence did not approach Urvashi. So both pined for each other. Urvashi was giving a dance performance in which she was portraying Vishnu's consort, Laxmi. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her concentration was with Pururava and she called out her lover's name instead of saying "Vishnu". Her teacher, the sage Bharat, got offended and cursed Urvashi. "You will get to live with the person you are thinking about," he s
2 CONC aid, "And you will also give birth to his son. But you will have to choose between the father and son, because the day they see each other you will have to leave them both and return to heaven."
2 CONT
2 CONT The curse actually emboldened Urvashi. She wasn't even thinking about children, she was sure to get her love. She sent a friend to earth to find out about Pururava. The friend located the king in the garden of Gandhmadan (meaning 
2 CONC intoxicating fragrance), whining away for his ladylove. The sage was set. Urvashi left heaven and went to the waiting arms of Pururava.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Urvashi From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia "Urvasi" redirects here. For the song, see Kadhalan (soundtrack). This article is about character in a Hindu legend. For other uses, see Urvashi (disambiguation). Urvashi Urvashi Urvas
2 CONC hi and Pururavas painted by Raja Ravi Varma Information Children	Rishyasringa from Vibhandaka Ayu from king Pururavas Urvashi (Sanskrit: उर्वशी, lit. she who can control heart of others. ("Ur" means heart and "vash" means to contr
2 CONC ol) is an Apsara (nymph) in Hindu legend. Monier Monier-Williams proposes a different etymology in which the name means 'widely pervasive' and suggests that in its first appearances in Vedic texts it is a name for the dawn goddess
2 CONC . She was a celestial maiden in Indra's court and was considered the most beautiful of all the Apsaras.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is mother of Rishyasringa great saint of the Ramayan era of ancient India from Vibhandaka who later played crucial role in birth of Rama and was married to Shanta elder sister of Ram.
2 CONT
2 CONT She became the wife of king Pururavas (Purūrávas, from purū+rávas "crying much or loudly"), an ancient chief of the lunar race. ShBr 11.5.1, and treated in Kalidasa's drama Vikramōrvaśīyam.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is perennially youthful and infinitely charming but always elusive.[1] She is a source as much of delight as of dolour.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Narayana on the left and Nara on the right, Deogarh, Uttar Pradesh, ca. 5th century There are many legends about the birth of Urvashi but the following one is most prevalent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once the revered sages Nara-narayana was meditating in the holy shrine of Badrinath Temple situated in the Himalayas. Indra, the king of the Gods, did not want the sage to acquire divine powers through meditation and sent two apsa
2 CONC ras to distract him. The sage struck his thigh and created a woman so beautiful that Indra’s apsaras were left matchless. This was Urvashi, named from ur, the Sanskrit word for thigh. After his meditation was complete the sage gif
2 CONC ted Urvashi to Indra, and she occupied the pride of place in Indra’s court.
