1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Madri was the sister of Salya, the king of Madhra. She became the second wife of Pandu, the King of the Kurus. She bore him the twins Nakula and Sahadeva, who were born by the grace of the Ashwini twins.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sahadeva
2 CONT
2 CONT Sahadeva as seen in the Javanese shadow puppet play (wayang) In the Hindu epic Mahabharata, Sahadeva (Sanskrit: सहदेव, sahadéva, lit. a thousand Gods) was the youngest of the five Pandava brothers. Nakula and Sahadeva were twins b
2 CONC orn to Madri, who had invoked the Ashwini Kumaras using Kunti's boon. Contents  [hide]  1 Birth and early years 2 Marriage 3 Conquest for Rajasuya 4 Exile 5 Role in the Kurukshetra War 6 Special Skills 7 Citations [edit]Birth and 
2 CONC early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to Pandu's inability to bear children (because of the curse of Rishi Kindama), Kunti had to use the boon given by Sage Durvasa to give birth to her three children. She shared the boon with Pandu's second wife, Madri, who invok
2 CONC ed the Ashwini Kumaras to beget Nakula and Sahadeva. Later Pandu lost his life when he attempted an intercourse with his wife, Madri. The latter also immolated herself in her husband's pyre, so Nakula and Sahadeva lost both their 
2 CONC parents at an early age. [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Later Kunti and the five Pandavas moved to Hastinapura. Like Nakula, Sahadeva's core skill lay in the wielding of the sword.[1] Sahadeva is said to be mild-mannered, bashful and virtuous.[2] All five Pandava brothers were wed conc
2 CONC urrently to Draupadi, and each had a son by her. Sahadeva's son with Draupadi was Srutakarma. Sahadeva also married Vijaya, the daughter of Dyutimat, the king of Madra, and had a son named Suhotra.[3] [edit]Conquest for Rajasuya
2 CONT
2 CONT Sahadeva's military expedition to the southern kingdoms, as per epic Mahabharata. Sahadeva was sent south by Yudhisthira to subjugate kingdoms for the Rajasuya sacrifice, after crowning as the Emperor of Indraprastha. He was speci
2 CONC fically chosen for the south because of his expertise with the sword, and because Bhishma opined that Southerners are skilled with sword-fighting in general.[4] The Mahabharata mentions several kingdoms to the south of Indraprasth
2 CONC a which were conquered by Sahadeva. Some of them are as under.[5] Surasenas Matsya, the king Dantavakra, kings Sukumara, Sumitra, other Matsyas and Patacharas. Vibhishana, the brother of Ravana and grandson of Pulastya. He offered
2 CONC  him diverse kinds of jewels and gems, sandalwood, celestial ornaments, costly apparel and valuable pearls. At Kishkindha, the monkey-kings Mainda and Dwivida were defeated in a 7-day war. City of Mahishmati, which was ruled by Ki
2 CONC ng Nila. Since the kingdom had the blessings of Agni, a huge fire obstructed the army when Sahadeva tried to invade; later a prayer to Agnideva enabled Sahadeva to complete the conquest. King Rukmi of Bhishmaka and territories of 
2 CONC Bhojakata Nishadas, the hill of Gosringa and King Sreenimath. Navarashtra, under King Kunti-Bhoja King Jamvaka, on the banks of the river Charmanwati. Territories lying on the banks of the Venwa. Kingdoms that lay on the banks of 
2 CONC the Narmada. Avanti, kings called Vinda and Anuvinda, town of Bhojakata King of Kosala King of Tripura King of Saurashtra Surparaka kingdom, Talakatas and Dandakas Mlechchha tribe living on the sea coast, Nishadas, the cannibals, 
2 CONC Karnapravarnas, and the Kalamukhas (a cross between human beings and Rakshasas), and the whole area of the Cole mountains Surabhipatna, and the island called the Copper island, and a mountain called Ramaka. The town of Timingila a
2 CONC nd a wild tribe known by the name of the Kerakas who were men with one leg. The town of Sanjayanti, countries of the Pashandas, Karahatakas, Paundrayas, Dravidas, Udrakeralas, Andhras, Talavanas, Kalingas and Ushtrakarnikas, Sekas
2 CONC  and Yavanas Paurava kingdom [edit]Exile
2 CONT
2 CONT Yudhisthira's loss in the game of dice meant that all Pandavas had to live in exile for 13 years. Once in exile, Jatasura, disguised as a Brahmin, kidnapped Nakula along with Draupadi, Sahadeva and Yudhisthira; Bhima rescued them 
2 CONC eventually. In the 13th year, Sahadeva disguised himself as a Vaishya and assumed the name of Arishtanemi (within themselves Pandavas called him Jayadbala) at the Kingdom of Virata.[6] He worked as a cowherd who supervised the mai
2 CONC ntenance and upkeep of all cows in Virata's kingdom. [edit]Role in the Kurukshetra War
2 CONT
2 CONT Sahadeva desired Virata to be the general of the Pandava army, but Yudhisthira and Arjuna opted for Dhristadyumna.[7] As a warrior, Sahadeva slew prominent war-heroes on the enemy side. The flag of Sahadeva's chariot bore the imag
2 CONC e of a silver swan.[8][9] During the gambling loss, he had taken an oath of slaying Shakuni. He accomplished this task successfully on the 17th day of battle. Among other prominent war-heroes killed by Sahadeva was Rukmaratha, the
2 CONC  son of Shalya.[10] After the war, Yudhisthira appointed Sahadeva as a personal bodyguard of his.[11] [edit]Special Skills
2 CONT
2 CONT Astrology: Sahadeva is said to be a great astrologer who prognosticated forthcoming events.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT ON THE 18TH DAY OF KURUKSHETRA WAR, SAKUNI LED THE ATTACK ON SAHADEVA'S DIVISION. AFTER A WHILE SAHADEVA DISCHARGED A SHARP-EDGED SWORD-ARROW SAYING "FOOL, HERE IS THE REWARD FOR YOUR GREAT SIN". IT WENT STRAIGHT AND CUT THROUGH S
2 CONC AKUNI'S NECK LIKE A SWORD AND THE HEAD, WHICH WAS AT THE FOOT OF ALL THE WICKED DEEDS OF THE KAURAVAS, ROLLED ON THE GROUND.
2 CONT
