2 CONC ays of the war when Karna was the commanded the battle forces. Vrasasena was killed by Arjuna. Vrushasena’s death is described in all its gruesome detail: Arjuna rubbed the string of his bow and took aim at Vrishasena in that batt
2 CONC le, and sped, O king, a number of shafts for the slaughter of Karna's son. The diadem-decked Arjuna then, fearlessly and with great force, pierced Vrishasena with ten shafts in all his vital limbs. With four fierce razor-headed ar
2 CONC rows he cut off Vrishasena's bow and two arms and head. Struck with Partha's shafts, the son of Karna, deprived of arms and head, fell down on the earth from his car, like a gigantic shala adorned with flowers falling down from a 
2 CONC mountain summit. Beholding his son, thus struck with arrows, fall down from his vehicle, the Suta's son Karna, endued with great activity and scorched with grief on account of the death of his son, quickly proceeded on his car, in
2 CONC spired with wrath, against the car of the diadem-decked Partha.
2 CONT
