1 NAME BHURISRAVAS /Somadutta/
2 GIVN BHURISRAVAS
2 _MARNM Somadutta
1 SEX M
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Killing of Bhurishravas POSTED ON SEPTEMBER 27, 2012 4
2 CONT
2 CONT Bhurishravaswas a prince of a minor kingdom in the kingdom of Bahlika and played a role in the Mahabharata epic.Bhurishravas’ father, Somadatta, once clashed with another prince called Sini. When Devaki, the mother of Lord Krishna
2 CONC  was still unwed, many princes competed for her hand in marriage including Somadatta and Sini who fought a great battle over her. Sini, fighting on behalf of Vasudeva won the battle. This incident launched an hatred between the Si
2 CONC ni and Somadatta families leading to a sibling rivalry. By the time of the Battle of Kurukshetra, Saini’s grandson Satyaki, now a king of the Vrishnis, was allied with the Pandava army while Bhurishravas, now aged, sided with the 
2 CONC Kauravas and was one of the eleven commanders of the Kaurava army. On the 14th day of the battle Bhurishravas challenged Satyaki. When Satyaki began to tire after a long and bloody battle, Bhurishravas battered him and dragged him
2 CONC  across the battlefield. Arjuna, the leading warrior on the Pandava army, was alerted to Satyaki’s danger by Lord Krishna. Just as Bhurishravas was preparing to kill Satyaki, Arjuna came to the rescue, shooting an arrow cutting of
2 CONC f Bhurishravas’ arm. Bhurishravas wailed that by striking him without a formal challenge, and from behind, Arjuna had disgraced the honor between warriors. Arjuna in turn rebuked Bhurishravas for attempting to kill an unarmed Saty
2 CONC aki – an act also against the rules of war. Arjuna reiterated that protecting Satyaki’s life at all costs was his responsibility as a friend and comrade in arms. At this point, realising his folly, Bhurishravas laid down his weapo
2 CONC ns, and sat in the lotus posture to practice yoga. But then Satyaki emerged from his swoon, and before Arjuna could stop him, swiftly decapitated his enemy. The warriors on both sides of the battle universally condemned Satyaki fo
2 CONC r this act – one of the incidents in the epic showing the superiority of dharma and honor against the uncontrollable power of hatred. Symbolically, as Bhurishravas’ attempt to kill the unarmed Satyaki immediately resulted in his o
2 CONC wn death in the same manner, Bhurishravas can be seen as representing the binding effects of one’s material actions (karma).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT AT THE SWAYAMWARA OF DEVAKA'S DAUGHTER DEVAKI.SOMADATTA WAS GREATLY ENRAGED, WHEN THE YADAVA SHINI ABDUCTED DEVAKI FOR THE BENEFIT OF VASUDEVA. A TERRIBLE FIGHT THEN TOOK PLACE IN WHICH SHINI CAUGHT HOLD OF SOMADATTA AND KICKED HI
2 CONC M IN THE PRESENCE OF ALL THE ATTENDING KINGS BUT THEN RELEASED HIM INSTEAD OF KILLING HIM. SOMADATTA BURNING  WITH A SENSE OF REVENGE, UNDERTOOK SEVERE PENANCES TO PLEASE MAHADEVA. FROM HIM HE OBTAINED A BOON TO HAVE A MIGHTY SON 
2 CONC WHO WOULD ONE DAY CATCH SHINI'S SON AND KICK HIM IN THE MIDST OF THOUSANDS OF KINGS. MAHADEVA GRANTED THIS BOON AND THUS BORN BHURISHRAVA. SOMADATTA HAD TWO OTHER SONS BHRI AND SHALA. IN THE GREAT MAHABHARATA WAR HE FOUGHT ON KAUU
2 CONC RAVA SIDE ALONG WITH HIS FATHER AND SONS AND WAS KILLED BY     SATYAKI.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 5th day of war bhurisravas kills 10 sons of Satyaki.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 14th day of the battle Bhurishravas was stationed in Dronacharya's Shakatavyuha(Cart Formation), attempting to stop Arjuna's from decimating King Jayathratha of Sindh. As Satyaki and Bhima came to support Arjuna, Bhurishrav
2 CONC as abandoned his position, and challenged Satyaki. Already tired from navigating the Shakatavyuha, Satyaki began to falter after a long and bloody battle. Their weapons destroyed, the fighting turned to hand-to-hand combat. Bhuris
2 CONC hravas pummeled Satyaki and dragged him across the battlefield when he was very tired and injured. Arjuna was alerted to Satyaki's danger by Lord Krishna. Just as Bhurishravas was preparing to kill Satyaki, Arjuna came to the resc
2 CONC ue, shooting an arrow cutting off Bhurishravas' arm.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bhurishravas wailed that by striking him without a formal challenge, and from behind, Arjuna had disgraced the honor between warriors. Arjuna in turn rebuked Bhurishravas for attempting to kill an unarmed Satyaki - an act also aga
2 CONC inst the rules of war. Arjuna also criticized Bhurishravas for partaking in the immoral the killing of Abhimanyu.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT At this point, realizing his folly, Bhurishravas laid down his weapons, and sat in the lotus posture to practice yoga.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT But then Satyaki emerged from his swoon, and before Arjuna could stop him, swiftly decapitated his enemy.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The warriors on both sides of the battle universally condemned Satyaki for this act[1] - one of the incidents in the epic showing the superiority of dharma and honor against the uncontrollable power of hatred.[2] Symbolically, as 
2 CONC Bhurishravas' attempt to kill the unarmed Satyaki immediately resulted in his own death in the same manner, Bhurishravas can be seen as representing the binding effects of one's material actions (karma).[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Years later, Bhurishravas's death would be used by Kritavarma to insult Satyaki. In the resulting fight, Satyaki (as well as the remaining Yadavas) perished.
