1 NAME SAGARA (VAIDARBHI) (SHAIBYA) /of Vedic period/
2 GIVN SAGARA (VAIDARBHI) (SHAIBYA)
2 _MARNM of Vedic period
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT -2750
1 TITL King
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ALTHOUGH KING SAGARA HAD TWO LOVELY QUEENS VAIDARBHI AND SHAIBYA, HE HAD NO CHILDREN. HE THEREFORE  UNDERTOOK AUSTERE PENANCES TO PLEASE THE GODS. AFTER MANY YEARS SHANKARA APPEARED BEFORE HIM AND SAID THAT ONE OF 
2 CONC HIS QUEENS WOULD HAVE SIXTY THOUSAND SONS BUT THEY ALL PERISH TOGETHER. THE OTHER QUEEN ACCORDING TO SANKARA WOULD HAVE ONLY ONE SON  BUT HE WOULD CONTINUE THE ROYAL LINEAGE. IN DUE COURSE QUEEN SHAIBYA GAVE BIRTH TO A SON ASMANJA
2 CONC SA BUT QUEEN VAIDARBHI GAVE BIRTH TO A BIG SQUASH, WHEN THE QUEEN WAS ABOUT TO THROW AWAY THIS SQUASH, A HEAVENLY VOICE FORBED  HIM   TO DO SO SAYING THAT HE SHOULD PRESERVE THE SEEDS OF THE SQUASH IN SEPARATE URNS. KING SAGARA FO
2 CONC LLOWED THESE INSTRUCTIONS FAITHFULLY ANS EVENTUALLY ONE SON WAS BORN FROM EACH OF THE SIXTY THOUSAND URNS.(3.106).                                                                                                                    
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT HE RECAPTURED AYODHYA FROM THE "HAIHAYA" AND "TALJANGHI" KSHTRIYAS. HE THEN ATTEMTED TO PERFORM THE HORSE SACRIFICE, ASHWAMEDHA YAJNA. HOWEVER,THE SACRIFICIAL HORSE WAS STOLEN BY GOD INDIRA ON THE SOUTH EASTERN SHORESOF THE OCEAN,
2 CONC  WHICH WAS AT THAT TIME AN EMPTY BED WITH NO WATER IN IT. AT LEAST SIXTY OF SAGARA'S SONS DIED ATTEMPTING TO RECOVER THE HORSE, ALSO CAUSING GREAT DESTRUCTION BY THEIR RECKLESS SEARCH.ASMANJA OR VARHIKETU- SAGARA'S SURVING SON WAS
2 CONC  NOT MADE KING DUE TO BAD CONDUCT. .  Sagara (Vedic king) Sagara, a Vedic King and ancestor of Rama. King Sagara is one of the greatest kings of theSuryavansha in the Satya Yuga, also known as theIkshvaku dynasty, he has two wives
2 CONC , one a princess of theVidarbha, and the other from royal lineage of Sivi [1] He was an ancestor to King Dasharatha and Lord Rama(Incarnation of Sree Vishnu).
2 CONT
2 CONT King Sagar’s great-great-grandson, Bhagiratha in penance.]Birth of Ganga King Sagara performed a horse sacrifice (Ashwamedha yajna) to prove his supremacy. Lord Indra, the leader of the demigods, became fearful over the results of
2 CONC  the yajna, so he decided to steal the horse. He left the horse at the ashram of Kapila, who was in deep meditation. King Sagar’s 60,000 sons, (born of Queen Sumati), and his sonAsamanja (born of Queen Keshini) were then sent to f
2 CONC ind the horse. When the 60,000 sons found the horse at Kapiladeva’s ashram, they thought he had stolen it. When they prepared to attack the meditating rishi (sage), Kapila opened his eyes. Because the sons of King Sagara had disre
2 CONC spected such a great personality, consequently, fire emanated from their own bodies, and they were immediately burned to ashes.[2] Later, King Sagara sent his grandson Amshuman to retrieve the horse. Kapiladeva returned the horse 
2 CONC and told Anshuman that the sons of King Sagar could be delivered if the Ganges descended to earth and bathed them in her waters. King Sagar’s great-great-grandson, Bhagiratha, eventually pleased MotherGanga, and asked her to come 
2 CONC to earth. Mother Ganga told Bhagiratha that the force of the Ganges falling from heaven would be too great for the earth to sustain, and that she needed someone to break the fall. Bhagiratha then worshiped Lord Siva, who then agre
2 CONC ed to accept the descending river upon his head. After the Ganga fell down on the ashes of the 60,000 sons of King Sagara they came alive and got their eternal position. [
2 CONT References 1.	^ Ikshaku tribe The Mahabharata translated by Kisari Mohan Ganguli (1883 -1896), Book 3: Vana Parva: Tirtha-yatra Parva: Section CVI, p. 228 'There was born in the family of the Ikshaku tribe, a ruler of the earth na
2 CONC med Sagara, endued with beauty, and strength...". 2.	^ Sons of Sagara Vishnu Purana translated by Horace Hayman Wilson, 1840, Book IV, Chapter IV.p. 378 the gods repaired to the Muni Kapila, who was a portion of Vishńu, free from 
2 CONC fault, and endowed with all true wisdom. Having approached him with respect, they said, "O lord, what will become of the world, if these sons of Sagara are permitted to go on in the evil ways which they have learned from Asamanja!
2 CONC  Do thou, then, assume a visible form, for the protection of the afflicted universe." "Be satisfied," replied the sage, "in a brief time the sons of Sagara shall be all destroyed."
